{
  "id": "Compound::DB00678 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "Losartan, a member of the Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonists class, is linked to Coronary Artery Disease through its inclusion in this class and its association with Valsartan and Telmisartan, both of which are also Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonists. The evidence suggests that these drugs may exert their therapeutic effects by modulating angiotensin II signaling, which is implicated in the pathogenesis of Coronary Artery Disease. The shared ancestry of Losartan with Valsartan and Telmisartan, along with their common links to the Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonist class, implies a potential mechanism of action involving vascular relaxation and reduction of arterial hypertension, both of which are risk factors for Coronary Artery Disease. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role of angiotensin II receptor antagonists in the treatment of coronary artery disease, supported by the hierarchical relationships and shared biological functions among these drugs.",
